C o m p i l a t i o n


      CEE 6410 - Water Resources Systems Analysis
      Example 2 from Lecture of Linear Problems and Graphical Problems
       
      THE PROBLEM:
       
      How many coups and minivans should a car manufacturer produce in a year?
      The data are as follows:
      • The car manufacturer profits by $6,000 per coup produced and $7,000 per
      minivan produced.
      • Each coup requires 1,000 pounds of metal to produce while each minivan requires
      2,000 pounds of metal.
      • The manufacturer has 4,000,000 pounds of metal available for the year.
      • Coups have more electronics and features. Thus, each coup requires 4 circuit
      boards per vehicle while each minivan requires 3 circuit boards per vehicle.
      • The manufacturer has purchased 12,000 circuit boards for the year.
      • One coup takes one worker 5 days to produce while one worker takes 2.5 days to
      produce a minivan.
      • The manufacturer has 67 workers and runs their factory for 261 days per year
      (i.e., the manufacturer has 17,500 worker-days per year).
       
      THE SOLUTION:
      Uses General Algebraic Modeling System to Solve this Linear Program
       
      David E Rosenberg
      david.rosenberg@usu.edu
      September 15, 2015
  29   
  30  * 1. DEFINE the SETS
  31  SETS veh crops growing /Coup, Minivan/
  32       res resources /Metal, CircuitBoards, Labor/;
  33   
  34  * 2. DEFINE input data
  35  PARAMETERS
  36     c(veh) Objective function coefficients ($ per vehicle)
  37           /Coup 6000,
  38          Minivan 7000 /
  39   
  40     b(res) Right hand constraint values (per resource)
  41            /Metal 4000000,
  42             CircuitBoards  12000,
  43             Labor  17500/;
  44   
  45  TABLE A(veh,res) Left hand side constraint coefficients
  46                   Metal    CircuitBoards   Labor
  47   Coup           1000             4          5
  48   Minivan        2000             3          2.5;
  49   
  50   
  51  * 3. DEFINE the variables
  52  VARIABLES X(veh) vehicles produced (Number)
  53            VPROFIT  total profit ($);
  54   
  55  * Non-negativity constraints
  56  POSITIVE VARIABLES X;
  57   
  58  * 4. COMBINE variables and data in equations
  59  EQUATIONS
  60     PROFIT Total profit ($) and objective function value
  61     RES_CONSTRAIN(res) Resource Constraints;
  62   
  63  PROFIT..                 VPROFIT =E= SUM(veh, c(veh)*X(veh));
  64  RES_CONSTRAIN(res) ..    SUM(veh, A(veh,res)*X(veh)) =L= b(res);
  65   
  66   
  67  * 5. DEFINE the MODEL from the EQUATIONS
  68  MODEL PRODUCTION /PROFIT, RES_CONSTRAIN/;
  69  *Altnerative way to write (include all previously defined equations)
  70  *MODEL PLANTING /ALL/;
  71   
  72   
  73  * 6. SOLVE the MODEL
  74  * Solve the PLANTING model using a Linear Programming Solver (see File=>Options=>Solvers)
  75  *     to maximize VPROFIT
  76  SOLVE PRODUCTION USING LP MAXIMIZING VPROFIT;
  77   
  78   
  79  * 6. CLick File menu => RUN (F9) or Solve icon and examine solution report in .LST file


